    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>AIM was founded in 1968 by Native American men who had served time in prison together, in a city where thousands of American Indians had moved under the relocation policy of the 1950s.</li><li>It used occupations, marches and the press to put treaty rights before the American public, from Alcatraz and Mount Rushmore to the Trail of Broken Treaties in 1972 and the 71-day occupation of Wounded Knee in 1973.</li><li>AIM founded schools, housing, job training, legal centres and the International Indian Treaty Council, but violence on Pine Ridge, FBI infiltration and a split in 1993 left it divided into rival groups.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1973 the actor Marlon Brando asked Sacheen Littlefeather to decline his Academy Award for him; she arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a> regalia and read his statement citing the poor treatment of Native Americans in the film industry.</p></aside>
    </section>

