      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1968</span><span class="rf-text">AIM is founded in Minneapolis, and the Minneapolis AIM Patrol is created to monitor police treatment of urban American Indians</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1970</span><span class="rf-text">On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanksgiving">Thanksgiving</a> Day an AIM group seizes the replica of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayflower">Mayflower</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, and AIM declares Thanksgiving a National Day of Mourning</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1971</span><span class="rf-text">Members occupy Mount Rushmore for a few days to protest the seizure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-hills">Black Hills</a>, and briefly occupy the headquarters of the Bureau of Indian Affairs in Washington</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1972</span><span class="rf-text">The Trail of Broken Treaties ends in the occupation of the Bureau of Indian Affairs headquarters, where the Twenty Points are publicised</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1973</span><span class="rf-text">About 300 Oglala Lakota and AIM activists occupy Wounded Knee, South Dakota, in a siege that lasts 71 days</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1975</span><span class="rf-text">Two FBI agents, Jack Coler and Ronald Williams, are killed on the Pine Ridge Reservation</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1978</span><span class="rf-text">The Longest Walk of 3,200 miles ends in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, and Congress passes the American Indian Religious Freedom Act</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1993</span><span class="rf-text">AIM splits into the Grand Governing Council and the International Confederation of Autonomous Chapters</span></li></ol>
